id: pm-gh1665
title: "GH-1665: extension flag contracts omit the SDK option key, so PmClient.run and the extension test harness silently ignore a CLI-spelled option such as status-filter and fall back to the default"
description: "GitHub issue #1665 (reported 2026-11-04 against 2026.11.4, found while authoring an extension from the published docs). An extension command declares --status-filter, reads options.statusFilter and defaults to open. On the CLI, pm demo-brief --status-filter closed returns closed items. Through PmClient.run('demo-brief', { options }) and through the extension test harness, options { statusFilter: 'closed' } returns closed items, but options { 'status-filter': 'closed' } silently returns the default open selection, with no refusal or warning. pm contracts --command demo-brief --flags-only --json lists --status-filter and nothing names the handler key statusFilter, so a caller copying the contract's spelling cannot learn the right key. Source is consistent with the report: the definition-driven extension option normalizer lives in src/cli/extension-command-options.ts and is consumed only by the CLI host (src/cli/main.ts and src/cli/runtime/invocation-options.ts), not by the SDK run path. Fleet packages work around it: the pm-brief package reads both include-history and includeHistory and accepts string booleans from direct callers. Expected: runtime flag contracts expose the handler key for every extension flag (for example sdk_option), and at the SDK boundary a known CLI-spelled option is normalized exactly as the host parser does, or refused naming the canonical key, with conflicting aliases refused. Why it matters for agents: an agent builds valid-looking context from the wrong lifecycle bucket and nothing tells it that its selection input was ignored. Related: pm-7lv8yx owns the inverse spelling leak (typed SDK refusals that name CLI flags), pm-11phn1 (GH-817) defined the invocation metadata that command_flags exposes, which has no option key, and pm-ill9gv (GH-719) routed host-owned globals to context.global. Duplicate check: searched extension flag option key, PmClient.run extension options, kebab and camel option normalization and harness parsed options across all statuses, and no item owns the extension flag-to-handler key mapping."
type: Issue
status: closed
priority: 1
tags[5]: agent-ux,contracts,extensions,"gh:1665",sdk
created_at: "2026-11-05T06:09:15.286Z"
updated_at: "2026-11-07T12:24:41.449Z"
closed_at: "2026-11-07T11:10:21.252Z"
completed_at: "2026-11-07T11:10:21.252Z"
author: "harness:claude-code"
estimated_minutes: 240
acceptance_criteria: "Every extension flag row in pm contracts --command <cmd> --flags-only --json and in pm help <cmd> --json carries the handler option key the command receives (for example sdk_option: statusFilter), derived from the same declaration the host parser uses; PmClient.run and the extension test harness normalize a declared CLI spelling such as status-filter to the handler key exactly as CLI dispatch does, or refuse with a typed error naming the canonical key, through one definition-driven normalizer shared with CLI dispatch; Passing two spellings of one declared flag with different values is refused with a typed conflict error naming both keys; A spec drives one extension command through CLI argv, PmClient.run with each spelling, and the harness, asserting the same selection, and a negative control that removes the SDK-side normalization fails it; docs/SDK.md and docs/EXTENSIONS.md state which key a direct caller passes and show the contract field"
parent: pm-ugqx
risk: medium
confidence: high
severity: medium
repro_steps: "Declare an extension command with --status-filter read as options.statusFilter (default open). pm demo-brief --status-filter closed returns closed. PmClient.run('demo-brief', { options: { 'status-filter': 'closed' } }) returns open with no warning"
resolution: "One definition-driven normalizer in src/sdk/extension/command-options.ts (moved from src/cli) now serves CLI argv dispatch, PmClient.run, MCP and runRegisteredCommandForTest. normalizeDirectExtensionCommandOptions folds each declared spelling (statusFilter, status-filter, --status-filter, a case-sensitive short letter) into the handler key, records direct values as attached occurrences so strict booleans are validated without the CLI positional fallback, refuses two spellings with different values with extension_option_spelling_conflict, then applies the shared defaults and coercion. pm contracts and help --json publish owner extension and sdk_option on every extension flag row, including extension rows on core commands. The shared coercer no longer lets a lower-cased short alias absorb another flag's own key. MCP tool-schema property names use the handler key. The normalizer and its definition type are exported from sdk/authoring and the aggregate. Migration: direct callers now receive declared defaults and coerced values, as CLI callers always did."
expected_result: "A CLI-spelled extension option passed to PmClient.run or the harness reaches the handler key exactly as on the CLI, conflicting spellings are refused, and contracts publish the key"
actual_result: "The integration spec drives one command through CLI argv, PmClient.run with three spellings and the harness and gets the same selection; conflicting spellings exit 2 with extension_option_spelling_conflict; contracts list sdk_option and owner; negative control (identity normalizer) fails the spec with selection undefined"
affected_version: 2026.11.4
component: extensions
